  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound` Explained\n\n## What is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an efficient algorithm for finding an item in a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (which takes O(n) time), binary search repeatedly divides the search interval in half:\n\n1. Look at the middle element.\n2. If the middle element equals your target, you're done!\n3. If the target is smaller, discard the right half.\n4. If the target is larger, discard the left half.\n5. Repeat until the target is found or the interval is empty.\n\nThis gives us **O(log n)** time complexity \u2014 dramatically faster than linear search for large lists.\n\n---\n\n## The `lower_bound` Function\n\n`lower_bound` returns the **index of the first element that is greater than or equal to** the target value. This is useful when:\n- You want to find where a value *should* be inserted in a sorted list.\n- You need the first occurrence of a duplicate value.\n- You need to determine if a value exists without necessarily returning its exact index.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List, TypeVar\n\nT = TypeVar('T',",
